## Proof

Let $H$ be a Hilbert space and $B(H)$ the bounded operators. We identify $B(H) \cong (L^{1}(H))^{*}$, where $L^{1}(H)$ denotes the trace-class operators, via the pairing $\langle T, A\rangle = \operatorname{Tr}(TA)$. The $w^{*}$-topology on $B(H)$ is the $\sigma$-weak (ultraweak) topology: $T_i \to T$ $w^{*}$ iff $\operatorname{Tr}(T_i A)\to \operatorname{Tr}(TA)$ for every trace-class $A$.

**Claim.** The $w^{*}$-closure of the finite-rank operators in $B(H)$ is all of $B(H)$.

If $\dim H < \infty$, every operator is finite rank, so the claim is trivial. Assume $\dim H = \infty$ and fix an orthonormal basis $\{e_n\}_{n\geq 1}$, with $P_m$ the projection onto $\operatorname{span}\{e_1,\dots,e_m\}$.

**Key lemma.** For every trace-class operator $A$, $\|A - P_m A P_m\|_{1} \to 0$ as $m\to\infty$.

*Proof of lemma.* Every trace-class operator admits a decomposition $A = \sum_{k} x_k \otimes y_k$ (where $(x\otimes y)(z)=\langle z,y\rangle x$) with $\sum_k \|x_k\|\|y_k\| < \infty$. For a rank-one summand,
$$\|(x_k\otimes y_k) - P_m(x_k\otimes y_k)P_m\|_{1} \le \|(I-P_m)x_k\otimes y_k\|_{1} + \|P_m x_k \otimes (I-P_m)y_k\|_{1} = \|(I-P_m)x_k\|\|y_k\| + \|x_k\|\|(I-P_m)y_k\| \to 0.$$
Summing and using dominated convergence (dominator $\sum_k 2\|x_k\|\|y_k\|<\infty$) gives $\|A - P_m A P_m\|_{1}\to 0$. $\square$

**Main argument.** Fix $T\in B(H)$ and set $F_m := P_m T P_m$, which is finite rank. For any trace-class $A$,
$$\operatorname{Tr}(F_m A) = \operatorname{Tr}(P_m T P_m A) = \operatorname{Tr}(T\, P_m A P_m),$$
using cyclicity of the trace (valid since $P_m T P_m$ is trace class). Therefore
$$|\operatorname{Tr}(F_m A) - \operatorname{Tr}(TA)| = |\operatorname{Tr}(T(A - P_m A P_m))| \le \|T\|\,\|A - P_m A P_m\|_{1} \xrightarrow{m\to\infty} 0.$$
Hence $F_m \to T$ in the $w^{*}$-topology. Since $T\in B(H)$ was arbitrary, every bounded operator lies in the $w^{*}$-closure of the finite-rank operators.

**Conclusion.** The $w^{*}$-closure of the finite-rank operators in $B(H)$ is the entire space $B(H)$. In particular, **yes**, this closure is all of $B(H)$.

